nvenc_drv.h (NVIDIA, SPDX MIT, "1993-2023") and clc5b7.h (NVIDIA, MIT): NVENC's picture-setup structures and class
methods from NVIDIA's open-gpu-doc (github.com/NVIDIA/open-gpu-doc, classes/video/, commit 9fdf5c40 2026-06-09).
Unmodified. nvenc_drv.h is written for class C9B7 but keeps one layout for every NVENC from MSENC on: its magic table
lists NV_NVENC_5_0 = 0xd0b70006 (T210's NVENC 5.0 firmware holds exactly that value) with the same revision byte as
every later class. The methods TegraNVENC uses have the same offsets in clc5b7.h, clc7b7.h and clc9b7.h. Struct sizes
match the header's own comments under clang (512-byte H.264 picture setup); test/nvenc_host checks them.
Used by TegraNVENCCheck.h and SwitchVideoEncoders/AVE.

Control values (SwitchVideoEncoders/AVE/H264NVENC.c, checked byte for byte by test/ave_host.c driverControls):
nvenc_drv.h documents the fields but not their values, and the first IDR sent with most of them 0 never finished (r7q,
TegraNVENCSelfTest.cpp step 3). The slice, ME, MD and quant controls, reference lists, slicing (slice_mode 0,
max_slice_size = macroblocks) and history size ((width_mbs * (height_mbs + 1)) * HIST_BLOCK_SIZE) are those of NVIDIA's
own T210 driver: its H.264 picture setups (magic 0xd0b70006, 1280x720 High profile CABAC 8x8) captured on a Switch and
published in github.com/TomasUribe/switch-frame-tap (GPL-2.0; data only, no code taken),
logs/m79-runE-setups/setup_3.bin (IDR; 8 and 9 identical) and setup_5.bin (P; 2, 4, 6, 7 identical), checked 2026-11-05,
decoded with this nvenc_drv.h. The two Ada NVENC hang causes found by github.com/mickys/nvenc (MIT)
docs/reports/T2.05e.md, slice_mode 1 and intra_ssd_cnt_* = 0, agree; NVENC 5.0's H.264 app does not read intra_ssd_cnt_*
(MD control bytes past 0x3a bits 0-2). What NVENC 5.0 reads was checked in the firmware (linux-firmware nvenc.bin,
envytools envydis -m falcon -V fuc5, the H.264 app at image offsets 0xc00-0xab00 = falcon addresses): setup at DMEM
0xa00 with the magic check at 0x8d96, ME control to DMEM 0x540 (read up to its mbc_mb_size word, written to I/O 0x1010
at 0x842a), MD to 0x600, quant to 0x1300, slice to 0x680 (per slice, 0x37c7-0x385a), hist_buf_size >> 7 to I/O 0xe0c
(0x3333).

End of an H.264 picture (tools/falcon, as SOURCE-H265): the app's ISR (0xf1b) adds I[0x1848] to DMEM 0x2e4 on each
encoder interrupt (IRQSTAT bit 13, I[0x1870] bit 1) and records errors in DMEM 0x2c8 (GP timer 0xe2640010, I[0x1870]
bit 0 0xe2640060, I[0x1ac4] bits 0-2 0xe2640200) without ending anything. fn 0x48b9 then spins at 0x4916-0x491b while
DMEM 0x2e4 < DMEM 0x354, with no timeout and no error check. DMEM 0x354 is the slice control's num_mb (0x38df),
clamped to the macroblocks left in the picture (0x38f7-0x390e) and given to the engine as I[0x191c] (0x39f5).

Picture size (tools/falcon flow_test): the app sizes the input fetch from input_cfg, (frame_*_minus1 + 1) >> 4
macroblocks, rounded down, into I[0xe88] (0x85d6-0x861b), and the picture from outputpic_cfg, (frame_*_minus1 + 16)
>> 4, rounded up, into DMEM 0x32c/0x32e, I[0x1808] (0x818c-0x81c3) and the slice target I[0x191c] (0x34d0). NVIDIA's
driver gives every surface the coded size (its 1920x1080 H.265 setup has frame_height_minus1 1087, SOURCE-H265), and
so does H264NVENC.c; the checker refuses any other. With 1080 the fetch stops a row short of the 68 macroblock rows the
engine encodes: the one slice never ends, the GP timer error lands with 0 of 8160 macroblocks counted (the ISR counts
when a slice ends, I[0x1870] bit 1 with the slice's bits in I[0x1ac4], 0xf6b-0xf8b) and the app spins at 0x4916 until
the kernel's job bound.

Where the OS keeps methods (tools/falcon on the OS overlay, 0x9f5-0xa51; the H.264 app reads them from DMEM 0x154, its
copy at 0x2e00): 0x700-0x74c are words 0-0x13 (0x780-0x7cc share them), with a written-bit mask at word 0x23 that
SET_APPLICATION_ID clears and the app's checks read (0x2f8d-0x3180); IN_REF_PIC0..15_LUMA are words 0x13-0x22. So on
NVENC 5.0 OUT_REF_PIC_CHROMA (0x74c) is reference slot 0, IN_REF_PIC0_CHROMA (0x440) overwrites the mask (E3's P
pictures' ucode error 0x120 on every boot of 11-06 is the app's 0xe2640120, CHROMA_V marked written but 0, which an
address bit in the mask gives), and 0x444-0x47c are dropped. The app
writes all 16 slots to I[0x1040-0x107c] on every picture (0x80a1, ME byte 0x8f bits 4-7 neither 1 nor 2, 0 here), and
NVIDIA's driver names slot 0 in every job it was captured sending (the other reconstruction), so H264NVENC.c's IDR does
too.

B pictures (libnvtvmr 32.7.6, the H.265 evaluation's library and method, see SOURCE-H265; H.264 create 0x30950, set
configuration 0x32e70, feed 0x31eb8, frame 0x2ce58; read and evaluated, never run). The caller states each picture's
type and, on an anchor, how many B pictures precede it in display order (feed record +0 and +8): an anchor's POC is the
previous anchor's plus 2 x (that count + 1) (0x2fa24-0x2fa58), each B's 2 more than the picture before (0x2efb8). A B
needs maxNumRefFrames >= 2 (0x2d0ac, create record +0xe) and is not a reference. Evaluated at 1280x720 High CABAC 8x8,
constant QP, maxNumRefFrames 2, in coding order I0 P2 B1 P4 B3 and I0 P3 B1 B2 P6 B4 B5, a B differs from the P before
it exactly in: pic_type 1 and ref_pic_flag 0; l0[0] and l1[0] the past and future anchors' slots << 1 (the H.264 app
gives the engine an entry's bits 1-4 as the slot and bit 0 as the parity, 0x895d-0x89a2 into I[0x1884], as app 3
does); temp_dist_l0[0] and temp_dist_l1[0] the POC differences / 2 (1 and -2, 2 and -1 in IBBP); dist_scale_factor[0][0]
by H.264 8-203 (85, 171; 128 in IBP) and every other scale factor -1 (a P's are all 256); frame_num the previous
reference's + 1; in the ME control predsrc.coloc_enable 1 and const_mv_stamp_l0/l1 3 (0x2ebd8-0x2ec3c); the slice QP.
MD control, quant control and the rest of the slice control are the P's. bdirect_mode is session configuration
(config +0x3c: 0 spatial, the default, 1 off, 2 temporal; 0x32f00-0x32f30) written into every picture (0x2e37c); the
captured P-only session has it off. An anchor's td_l0 is its distance to the previous anchor (3 in IBBP).
Methods (the P's set plus): every picture names OUT_COLOC_DATA, the colocated buffer paired with its reconstruction
(0x2e8c4-0x2e8f0); a B names IN_COLOC_DATA, its list-1 reference's (0x2efdc, 0x30260), and IN_REF_PIC<slot>_LUMA for both
references (0x2e784-0x2e884, one method a reference on chip 6). Buffers: maxNumRefFrames + 1 reconstructions with a
colocated buffer each of (macroblocks x 64 + 255) & ~255 bytes (0x30dd4-0x30e14); a picture is coded into the one
holding no picture of the DPB, a new anchor takes the slot of the one it evicts (anchor n in slot n & 1).
Firmware: the app copies the method words to DMEM 0x380 (0x2e41-0x2ece), IN_COLOC_DATA at 0x3a4 and OUT_COLOC_DATA at
0x3a8. Non-zero IN_COLOC_DATA goes to I[0xd00] with the macroblock count in I[0xd04], non-zero OUT_COLOC_DATA to
I[0x1864] with the size in macroblocks in I[0x98c]/I[0x990] (0x333e-0x3398); a zero value leaves those registers as
they were. With IN_COLOC_DATA the ME's colocated hint (I[0xd0c]) is the ME control's coloc_enable for a P or B, off
for an I or IDR; with OUT_COLOC_DATA the engine writes colocated motion (I[0x1860]) for every picture but a B
(0x5866-0x58b0). The checks: IN_COLOC_DATA written as 0 in a P or B with coloc_enable, or OUT_COLOC_DATA written as 0
in an I, IDR or P, is error 0xe2640180 (0x311a-0x319e). The app reads l1 at 0x8a01-0x8a62 and temp_dist_l1 into
I[0x478] (0x83b4-0x83d9).

Input pictures (tools/falcon accesses with --region 0xa24:0x20, input_cfg at setup + 0x24; both apps alike, H.264 at
0x85d3-0x86d5, H.265 at 0x1411b-0x141ee): of the surface config the apps read the size, both pitches, sfc_trans_mode,
the four offsets, byte 0x40 (block_height bits 0-6, tiled_16x16 bit 7) and bits 0-1 of byte 0x41 (memory_mode); no
app reads nv21_enable or input_bl_mode. I[0xe88] gets width and height in macroblocks (bits 0-8, 9-17), sfc_pitch >> 4
(9 bits from 18: at most 8176 bytes), tiled_16x16 at bit 27 and memory_mode at bits 29-30 (0x85d3-0x861b); I[0xeb8]
sfc_pitch_chroma >> 4 (0x8622); I[0xeac] sfc_trans_mode & 7 (0x8633); I[0xe8c] a block-height code, block_height
32 -> 4, 16 -> 3, 8 -> 2, 4 -> 1, anything else 0 (0x8643-0x8664); I[0xe80] luma_top_frm_offset + IN_CUR_PIC
(0x8686-0x8695); I[0xe84] chroma_top_frm_offset + CHROMA_U for memory_mode 0 and 2, else planar input with
I[0xeb0] chroma_top_frm_offset + CHROMA_U and I[0xeb4] chroma_bot_offset + CHROMA_V (0x8698-0x86d5). No bit picks
pitch linear: a surface is 16x16 tiles or block linear by its block-height code, block_height 0 and 2 giving the same
code 0.
NVIDIA's T210 driver (libnvtvmr, above) at create sets input_cfg memory_mode 1 and byte 0x40 to 0x80, 16x16 tiles
(0x311a8-0x311b8); per picture (0x2d4cc-0x2d520), for an input NvRmSurface of layout 3 (Blocklinear, +0x10) it clears
the chroma offsets and sets block_height to 1 << BlockHeightLog2 (+0x24), masked to 7 bits, and tiled_16x16 0; for
every layout memory_mode becomes 1 when the chroma plane's colour format's low byte is 8, else 0, and nv21_enable 0;
any other layout keeps the 16x16 tiles.
TVMRVideoSurfaceCreate (0x42a8) makes each plane an NvRmSurface: layout 3 at +0x10, kind 0xfe at +0x20 and
BlockHeightLog2 at +0x24 for a block-linear surface (0x43fc-0x4414), BlockHeightLog2 1 unless the caller's flags bits
6-8 name 1..5 (0x43a4-0x43c0, 0x4508-0x4514), layout 2 for the 16x16-tiled one (0x44c4-0x44d0); pitch rows are 64-byte
multiples (0x441c-0x4430). So block_height counts 64Bx8 GOBs, code k is 2 << k GOBs, and NVIDIA's own block-linear
surfaces reach NVENC as block_height 2, code 0: code 0 reads 2-GOB blocks, the layout NVDEC writes. NVIDIA's stack
hands NVENC 16x16 tiles or block linear and never pitch linear, which no field selects. HW Test
encode.nvenc_input_layouts checks this on the unit, one IDR from each layout decoded back.
